    "title": "The Farewell: Joey Jordison's Final Slipknot Studio Album",
    "content": "Released on August 26, 2008, *All Hope Is Gone* is Slipknot's fourth studio album — and, though no one knew it at the time, Joey Jordison's last studio record with the band that defined his career. Recorded with producer Dave Fortman at Sound Farm Studios outside Des Moines, Iowa — the first Slipknot album recorded in the band's home state — *All Hope Is Gone* debuted at #1 on the Billboard 200, hit #1 in the UK and Australia, and was certified Gold within months. The single \"Psychosocial\" earned Slipknot a Grammy nomination for Best Metal Performance, becoming one of the most recognisable songs in the band's catalogue.\n\nBy 2008, Joey Jordison had become one of the most decorated metal drummers in the world. Voted #1 in Roadrunner's \"Greatest Drummers of the Last 25 Years\" poll the following year, he entered the *All Hope Is Gone* sessions with the full weight of his reputation behind him — and a gear setup that reflected his evolution from the Tama Rockstar of 1999, through the Pearl Masterworks of Iowa, to the Pearl Reference Series that became his late-career flagship kit. The Reference Series' maple/birch hybrid shell construction gave Joey a tonal palette better suited to the more refined, melody-conscious arrangements that Fortman's production foregrounded. Where Ross Robinson's Iowa sessions had buried the kit in a wall of noise, and Rick Rubin's Vol. 3 had exposed every dynamic detail, Fortman's *All Hope Is Gone* recordings strike a middle path: clarity in the service of brutality.\n\nThe drumming on *All Hope Is Gone* shows a player at the height of his discipline. \"Psychosocial\" — the Grammy-nominated single — is built on a double-bass pulse that locks the verse riff with mechanical precision while Joey's groove-conscious hi-hat work keeps the song from collapsing into pure aggression. \"Gematria (The Killing Name)\" opens the album with one of his most demanding sustained double-bass performances. \"Dead Memories\" reveals the melodic restraint he was capable of in service of a song. Tracks like \"Sulfur\" and \"Vendetta\" showcase the wider tom configuration of the Reference Series setup — fills that breathe and orchestrate rather than simply blast.\n\nPaul Gray's bass playing influence on the production cannot be overstated. Gray — who would tragically die in 2010 — co-wrote much of the album and pushed for a thicker low-end recording approach that gave the rhythm section more weight than any previous Slipknot record. This shaped the bass-drum tuning, the snare tuning depth, and the overall mix philosophy Fortman applied at Sound Farm.\n\nIn hindsight, *All Hope Is Gone* is the arc closer of Jordison's recorded Slipknot legacy: Self-Titled (1999) → Iowa (2001) → Vol. 3 (2004) → All Hope Is Gone (2008). His departure from the band would come in 2013, and his death — at 46 — in July 2021. This article documents the gear that powered his farewell Slipknot studio performance.",
